s unique presumably. But environ.py has already been modified — maybe by a previous partial edit? changed_files includes environ.py, so it was modified. Perhaps the previous request already added the config item and maybe a helper. Let me read that region to verify.\n\nI'll structure this action:\n1. print environ lines 1-150 (descriptor class).\n2. print search for \"MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\" in environ.py.\n3. Print whether environ logging section already has it: read_range 195-215.\n\nThen next action: all edits + pytest. Tight.\n\nAlternatively do the environ edit in this same block guarded: check if name absent, then apply replace_text. I can write code:\n\n```python\nc = read_range(\"deepinfer/environ.py\",1,150)\nprint(c['content'])\n```\n\nand separately try the replace based on presence check:\n\n```python\nenv_full = read_file(\"deepinfer/environ.py\")  # full read again - not needed\n```\nActually read_file returns full content and hash; re-reading registers evidence but full reads already registered for environ? full_reads_registered only lists logger.py and stats_logger.py — environ.py full read maybe earlier (ev-... hash eea533...). Fine.\n\nLet me do in this block:\n- read_range environ 1-150 and print\n- if \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS\" not in environ content: apply replace_text inserting config after DEEPINFER_LOGGING_INTERVAL_STEP line, with a validator factory.\n\nBut I need the descriptor class internals to write a correct validator. I can write the validator without the class: use EnvVar(lambda ...)? The class EnvVar likely has pattern: EnvVar(int_type?) Let's see from evidence: `EnvVar.int(default=...)`, `EnvVar.str()`, `EnvVar.float(...)`, `EnvVar.bool()`, `EnvVar.choice(...)`. So EnvVar(lambda v: ...) is likely constructor taking a function. E.g. `DEEPINFER_ENGRAM_FORCE_GPU_STORAGE = EnvVar(lambda v: _bool(v) or envs.DEEPINFER_ACCELERATOR == \"ascend\")`. So EnvVar(func) works.\n\nSo I can write:\n\n```python\ndef _log_max_message_chars(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    try:\n        value = int(v)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n        raise ValueError(f\"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS must be an integer >= 32, got {v!r}\") from None\n    if value < 32:\n        raise ValueError(...)\n    return value\n```\n\nBut where to put it? Need location relative to _bool etc. I could put the helper right above the Config class... Simplest: define it as a lambda inside the class field:\n\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(lambda v: _parse_log_max_message_chars(v))\n```\n\nand define `_parse_log_max_message_chars` above the class. Without knowing exact structure, I can insert the helper function right before the class definition line if I knew it. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: write the whole validation inline in a lambda — ugly but works:\n\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(lambda v: None if v is None else (_ for _ in ()).throw(...))\n```\nToo hacky.\n\nBetter approach: I'll insert the helper definition immediately after the logging section? Module-level function defined after class is fine as long as it's resolved at call time (lambda references module global). Yes! The lambda calls `_parse_log_max_message_chars(v)` at access time, by which point the module is fully loaded. So I can insert the config line in the Logging section and the helper function after rearm_all (at module end). Clean.\n\nBut wait: does environ.py define `envs = environ.Env()`? The descriptors are set as class attributes on something... `setattr(envs, name, _descriptors[name])` — so envs is an instance of a class with slots? And `_descriptors` collected. Descriptors must be class attributes... setattr on instance overrides. Fine — as long as I add the class attribute the same way others are, works.\n\nHmm, but if descriptors are registered via a metaclass scanning class body, my added line in the class body gets registered. Good.\n\nRisk: setting a different class attribute name for the helper — the helper must be OUTSIDE the class body (module level) so it isn't treated as a descriptor. Placing after rearm_all is outside the class. Good.\n\nBut careful: does the class body capture `_descriptors` with EnvVar assignments only? Presumably metaclass collects EnvVar instances. A plain function assigned in class body might be treated as descriptor? I'm not putting it in class body, so fine.\n\nNow logger.py edits. I have the full NewLineFormatter and _setup_logger from evidence.\n\nNewLineFormatter __init__ signature: add keyword-only param `max_message_chars: int | None = None`. Note existing 4 positional params. Requirement: \"保留原有四个位置参数，新参数 max_message_chars 只能按名称传入\" → add `*,` after use_color.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\n_OMIT_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\ndef _validate_max_message_chars(value):\n    if value is None:\n        return None\n    if isinstance(value, bool) or not isinstance(value, int):\n        raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {value!r}\")\n    if value < 32:\n        raise ValueError(...)\n    return value\n```\n\nformat():\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n```\nNeed to budget only message body, not prefix/exception/stack_info. Approach: compute the truncated `record.message` before calling super().format? Careful: super().format uses record.getMessage() to compute message; we must not modify record.msg/args. Approach: temporarily compute message ourselves.\n\nPlan:\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        keep_full = getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True\n        message = record.getMessage()  # after %s substitution\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and not keep_full:\n            message = self._truncate(message)\n            original_message = record.message\n            original_attrs = ...\n```\nBut super().format() calls record.getMessage() itself and sets record.message. To feed truncated text without mutating msg/args, I can:\n- save `record.msg`, `record.args`? Not allowed to rewrite them (means must not permanently change). Temporary change then restore is \"改写共享原记录\" — risky. Requirement says \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\". Safer: override formatting by computing the whole thing myself? Or use `record.message` trick: logging.Formatter.format does:\n\n```python\nrecord.message = record.getMessage()\nif self.usesTime(): s = self.formatMessage(record)\n...\n```\nIt always recomputes record.message. So I can't just set record.message.\n\nAlternative: create a shallow copy of the record via `logging.makeLogRecord(dict(record.__dict__))`? Then set copy.message... but format() still recomputes record.message = getMessage(). However, if I set copy.msg = truncated_message and copy.args = (), then getMessage returns the truncated message — the copy is private, not the shared original. That satisfies \"不改写共享原记录\".\n\nBut then exception formatting etc. works on the copy (exc_info copied by reference, fine). Time formatting fine.\n\nHowever, requirement 5: \"沿用原有多行前缀、时间精度、颜色及异常处理\" — using copy is fine.\n\nBut careful: formatter.format also sets record.message, record.exc_text maybe. Since we pass copy, original untouched. Good.\n\nAlso multi-line prefix logic uses `record.message` from the copy — but if truncated, the prefix computation uses original record.message... In format code: `if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message: prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]`. With copy, record.message is truncated message; but msg contains truncated message only if format used the truncated... Actually we compute prefix from full msg which embeds the truncated message. `msg.find(record.message)` with copy works since copy.message == truncated. Good.\n\nTruncation math: budget B, marker length M=15. remaining = B - M. head = ceil(remaining/2), tail = remaining - head. Requirement: \"开头保留向上取整的一半，末尾保留其余部分\" — 例如预算32时，保留开头9字